[X] 1. Keep the normal data path on the data queue, not the management queue.

[X] 2. Delay firmware load until interface up.
Load firmware, read efuse/MAC, and do runtime MAC/RX bring-up from
`urtwx_init()` instead of attach so autoconf does not depend on `/etc/firmware`.

[X] 3. Fix the intermittent `!chan,!bssid` association race.
This still smells like an async state transition ordering/timing bug.

[X] 4. Fix RSSI / RX PHY reporting.
The current 0% RSSI / weak PHY reporting is not just cosmetic; it affects
scan quality, BSS selection, and later 5 GHz debugging.

[X] 5. Validate long-run WPA/RSN stability.
WPA2 traffic is stable under GTK rekey stress, reconnect loops, AP
reboot/loss recovery, mode transitions, and a 60+ minute soak.

[X] 6. Expose real 802.11n/HT on 2.4 GHz.
HT20 traffic is working, firmware rate adaptation is active, and throughput is
measurably better than 11g.

[X] 7. Report live firmware RA / TX-rate changes to `net80211`.
The firmware is deciding real runtime TX rates, and `ifconfig` now tracks
legacy vs HT mode changes through C2H RA report updates.

[X] 8. Bring up 5 GHz in stages.
5 GHz 11a and HT are working on non-DFS channels for both open and WPA2.

[X] 9. Add wide-channel support.
HT40 is working on both 2.4 GHz and non-DFS 5 GHz for open and WPA2.

[X] 10. Add 802.11ac / VHT support.
VHT80 is working on non-DFS 5 GHz for both open and WPA2. Throughput tuning
still depends on on-air aggregation work.

[X] 11. Add PMF / MFP support.
PMF now works with both optional and required AP policy, and unprotected
deauthentication no longer disconnects the station.

[~] 12. Add on-air TX aggregation.
TX BA negotiation and descriptor aggregation hints are working and give a
small throughput improvement, but gains are still modest and likely limited
by the current USB TX model.
If this is revisited, the next likely step is a per-node/per-TID TX scheduler
rather than more USB batching tweaks. OpenBSD `net80211` currently exposes BA
state but not a generic `mac80211`-style TXQ framework, so the practical first
implementation would likely live in `urtwx`; if that proves useful, the more
general queueing/scheduling pieces could be considered for promotion into
`net80211` later.

[X] 13. Add on-air RX aggregation.
RX BA is working at `URTWX_RX_LIST_COUNT = 16` and reverse multi-stream
throughput is now stable in the ~50 MB/s range. `urtwx` currently disables
`A-MSDU in A-MPDU` in RX BA agreements because allowing it causes severe
reorder-path collapse on this USB design.

[~] 14. Harden USB recovery and error handling.
Validation is mostly good: repeated `down/up`, open<->WPA2 churn, and unplug
during scan/assoc/traffic all recovered cleanly in testing. One rare
unplug-during-scan case failed to detach, but that may be generic OpenBSD USB
stack behavior rather than a `urtwx`-specific bug. Still not explicitly
validated:
- watchdog-triggered TX timeout recovery
- RX/TX endpoint stall recovery under load
- any suspend/resume style stale-state path that shows up on USB

[ ] 21. Add DFS station-channel support.
Useful mainly for compatibility and usability on 5GHz networks which auto-pick
DFS channels. This should be treated as a station-mode regulatory/channel
exposure task, not a throughput feature.

[ ] 15. Add support for other RTL88xx USB parts.
Start with closely related 8821/8822 variants after 8821CU is stable enough
that refactoring will not move a still-changing target.

[ ] 16. Consider PCI `rtwx` support.
Likely worth waiting until there is test hardware and the shared 88xx code is
less fluid.

[ ] 17. Evaluate 2T2R / MIMO work.
The current RTL8821CU work is 1T1R; MIMO likely belongs with item 14.

[ ] 18. Improve comments and function preambles in `if_urtwx.c` and
`if_urtwxreg.h`.

[X] 19. Write `walkthrough.md`.
Document firmware loading, reserved pages, the security engine, scan/assoc
bring-up, and the main differences from the Linux `rtw88` path.

[X] 20. Write `urtwx(4)`.
Follow the same pattern and style as other OpenBSD USB Wi-Fi driver manpages.
